<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A derivation declares a build-only tool such as <code>cmake</code>,
<code>meson</code>, or <code>pkg-config</code> in <code>buildInputs</code>.
In nixpkgs, tools that run during the build normally belong in
<code>nativeBuildInputs</code> so they target the build platform rather than the
package's host platform.
</p>
<p>
The distinction matters most when cross-compiling, and also in packages that
set <code>strictDeps = true;</code>. Leaving build tools in
<code>buildInputs</code> can make the package harder to cross-build and obscures
which dependencies are true runtime inputs.
</p>
<p>
There are exceptions, such as IDE or developer environments that intentionally
need the tool available at runtime. In those cases, keeping the tool in
<code>buildInputs</code> can be reasonable; otherwise it should move.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Move the tool from <code>buildInputs</code> to <code>nativeBuildInputs</code>.
Keep ordinary runtime libraries such as <code>openssl</code> in
<code>buildInputs</code>.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic — a build tool is declared in <code>buildInputs</code>:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv, cmake }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  buildInputs = [ cmake ];
}
</sample>
<p>Recommended — build tools belong in <code>nativeBuildInputs</code>:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv, cmake }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  nativeBuildInputs = [ cmake ];
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/build-tools-in-build-inputs.md">
<code>nixpkgs-hammering</code> explanation: <code>build-tools-in-build-inputs</code></a>
</li>
<li>
<a href="https://nixos.org/manual/nixpkgs/stable/#chap-cross">Nixpkgs manual: cross compilation</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
